# Fast Food Cooking — Implementation and Verification

Target: single-player Fast Food in Lobby2. Updated 2026-09-25.

## Agreed gameplay

Help Kitchen opens Grill, Fry, and Assembler selection. One station is active at a time, using a focused first-person perspective and the existing Restock pointer/3D-preview conventions. Station interactions operate on shared restaurant state.

Occupied-station ownership replaces the earlier one-in-ten quota. Staff finish only work they already started before entry. All unstarted/new work at the occupied station belongs to the player, including all new Assembler tickets. One batch or ticket is actionable at a time; the remaining work queues without running its player timer. Leaving or switching hands unfinished work to staff without resetting progress. Other stations continue automatically. A player batch can contain six servings, cooked in waves using four player positions; burger preparation adds ingredients one at a time.

Staff own the left grill (four positions) and the left two fryer wells (two positions each). Players own the right grill (four positions) and right two fryer wells (two positions each). At the occupied station, staff finish pre-entry jobs but start no new jobs. Leaving hands over already-started player food in place rather than restarting it.

Back to Stations and Exit Kitchen cancel an uncommitted drag and hand committed work to staff without restarting timers. Normal game pauses, hygiene pauses, and the existing Restock scene pause remain authoritative.

## Production and assembly

Grill owns burger cooking and burger/sandwich preparation. Load patties into the available player positions. Ready proteins transfer to holding preparation, the camera shifts to the preparation table, and the player adds each recipe ingredient. Chicken and fish sandwich fillings are cooked by Fry and handed to Grill preparation; the status explains that handoff. Only the completed product counts.

Fry owns fries, nuggets, fried chicken, and chicken/fish sandwich fillings. Drag a serving into a free player position, wait for Ready, then pick it up: the perspective camera moves to the prep table for collection. Loading raw food never triggers this camera move. Completed food visibly stages at saved table anchors. Sandwich fillings are identified as Grill handoffs and leave the fryer display after Grill finishes them. Portions follow the menu recipe, including foods consisting of several pieces.

Cooking has a Ready window followed by Burnt. Discarding unfinished burnt food releases its reservation and retries without consuming ingredients. Cooking/overcook durations and staff speed are editable.

Assembler displays only the player's active ticket, with product images, placed/required counts and a timer. Drag completed servings and drinks onto the physical tray. Drinks fill instantly on valid placement. The green Serve button appears when all requirements are present. Wrong/excess drops return safely. Staff orders run in the background; staff finish handed-over trays.

Batches aggregate production, not customer tickets. Progress counts completed products, and serving food does not reverse that count. Targets stay fixed after production begins, except cancellation removes abandoned demand. The default reserve is two servings per available food product, generated while paid orders are active. Deadlines do not steal player work while the station is occupied and add no fine.

## Inventory rules

There is one inventory authority.

- Acceptance and cooking preparation reserve ingredients without consuming them.
- Available portions exclude reservations internally. The hotbar shows remaining inventory units, which are debited only when a finished product consumes them.
- Completing a full burger, finished fried serving, or filled drink atomically consumes recipe ingredients once.
- Assembly and order delivery do not debit the raw ingredients again.
- Cancelled or burnt unfinished work releases reservations without a raw-stock charge.
- Finished cancelled food returns to ready stock.
- Player and staff cannot reserve the same portion or finish the same order twice.
- Remake order numbers transfer the existing reservation rather than creating another one.

The hotbar follows the compact Restock design: one ingredient icon and a bottom-right xN count of remaining units. No item-name paragraphs, box counts, reservation breakdowns or stock-status rows. Assembly cells show xN outstanding servings for the player's ticket. There is no separate box-opening system.

Fast Food uses completion-time consumption; other restaurant modes keep their existing acceptance-time behavior. Existing KitchenManager delivery remains authoritative, with its old automatic cooking delay bypassed for orders awaiting this assembly flow.

Finished food is included in the existing restaurant save data. Unfinished Fast Food sessions continue to roll back through the existing day-start checkpoint. This change does not introduce mid-shift customer recovery or a new save system. Completed stock is retained across normal service resets.

## HUD and visual rules

Reuse existing Restock panel sprites, Anton font assets, ingredient icons, and product images. Use the saved cooking/serving models and existing glass drink geometry. Fries and nuggets use cooked icons in the kitchen hotbar for recognition; inventory counts still represent their raw supply units.

Use natural Blue sprites for backgrounds and Grey sprites for foreground cards/panels. Use dark blue (#093D59) Anton text on Grey surfaces and white Anton text on other backgrounds. A colored button inside a Grey card keeps white text. Green means confirm/positive, Red means cancel/negative, and Yellow means warning. Keep UI Image tint white. Saved kitchen sprite assets reuse the original textures with consistent slice borders; do not alter the shared source textures or recolor their artwork. Food icons retain their original colors. Sprite choices, button states, text material and layout are saved in Lobby2 and the UI prefabs, editable in the Inspector, and are never reapplied automatically during gameplay.

- Selection shows the three stations without staff workload text.
- A compact station header appears only for active player work and a brief completed-batch display. Task/staff cues use a deduplicated, bounded floating lane with food icons: white normal, red errors, yellow warnings, green success, Anton with a saved black-outline material and no background containers.
- A centered, single-row bottom hotbar fits its cells and shows unlocked station ingredients or the player's outstanding assembly items; larger menus scroll horizontally within the Inspector's maximum width.
- Staff models and nameplates are hidden only during first-person station play and restored on Back/Exit/disable. Staff simulation continues; the saved visual-reference lists are editable in the Inspector.
- Use the real appliance surface instead of covering it with a dark proxy slab. Preparation/tray targets remain physical; matching drop hints appear while dragging, and discard appears only for burnt food.
- Assembler's saved camera and target face the actual prep countertop; assembly and fryer collection reuse the existing Food Tray mesh/material. KitchenInteraction isolates station pointer hits from hidden staff and surrounding appliance colliders.
- Preview copies are centered around their combined mesh bounds and use a saved translucent ghost material, green for valid and red for invalid drops. The underlying food assets are not recolored. Outlines retain the shared Restock preparation helper.
- Invalid drops animate back toward their source.
- The optional existing task HUD shows only the active station's next step.
- Lobby tasks cannot displace station guidance, even at higher priority.
- Global restock needs appear through a compact Grey button only when relevant; details open on request in a Grey card within the Blue panel, with a Green Go to Restock action. Locked or disabled menu ingredients do not produce stock warnings.
- Go to Restock exits kitchen help safely and uses the existing restock navigation.
- Unrelated lobby controls are hidden during kitchen help and restored on exit.

No permanent Staff Working card, duplicate pointer-instruction bar or empty order board. Cooking timers appear beside active food. Optional step guidance uses the existing task HUD, with no idle staff-status task. The compact layout is authored using Dine In > Fast Food > Simplify Kitchen Presentation and saved to Lobby2 and the existing UI prefabs; it is never laid out from scratch during gameplay.

The kitchen simulation does not depend on a station panel being visible.

## Cozy feedback, without extra chores

- Valid drops settle into their saved food anchors with a short placement animation and quiet sound. Invalid drops return safely without an ingredient penalty.
- Cooking gradually warms the food's existing material colors; a soft chime and circular progress indicator mark Ready. Small grill steam and fryer bubbles stay close to the food; fryer food sits slightly below the oil.
- Tickets slide/fade in on assignment and out on removal, retaining current product pictures and counts. Their slim striped progress bars match the main HUD style. The larger product viewport accommodates two rows without clipping. Restock and station panels keep their short reveal/press feedback.
- Saved left/right buttons cycle Grill, Fry and Assembler with a brief fade. Exit cancels an in-progress transition. Cook/prep motion interpolates the user's perspective camera poses. The assembler faces the clear working side of its table, with an enlarged tray and spaced item anchors.
- Saved drink prefabs stand upright and contain translucent glass shells and individual Coke, Pineapple Juice and Iced Tea liquid materials/volumes.
- Completed prep remains visible briefly before the next assignment. Instructions describe only the next action, and cross-station messages explain where sandwich fillings are coming from.
- Reduced Motion skips placement/return motion and camera interpolation. Audio is routed to the existing SFX mixer and can be replaced or tuned in the Inspector.
- No scoring pressure, precision gestures, new currency, mandatory minigames or additional stock systems are added.

## Implementation layout

- FastFoodCookingState.cs: reservations, production stages, batches, staff work, tickets and shared ready food.
- FastFoodCookingController.cs: saved Lobby2 controller, real order/payment integration, inventory transactions, editable guidance and save hooks.
- FastFoodCookingView.cs: runtime binding to saved UI, station rigs, templates and pointer handling. No runtime UI, camera, surface or material construction.
- FastFoodCookingView.Authoring.cs: Editor-only initial layout creation, excluded from player builds.
- FastFoodCookingView.Feedback.cs: placement feedback, ready sounds and gradual cooking color changes using the existing food models.
- FastFoodCozyPolishAuthoring.Gameplay.cs: explicit Editor-only saving of owned positions, effects, compact UI, glass drinks and sound assets.
- FastFoodCookingStation.cs: serialized cameras, targets, anchors, food/preview templates and interaction colors.
- FastFoodCookingDragHandle / FastFoodCookingTicketView / FastFoodCookingDropTarget: saved template and interaction bindings.
- Existing order acceptance, KitchenManager, stock bridge, task guidance, staff visuals and save hooks are extended.
- RestockRoomController.PrepareWorldDragPreview is shared between the existing Restock interaction and cooking.
- FastFoodCookingAuthoring creates the complete persistent hierarchy once and validates it; build validation rejects a Lobby2 without its authored kitchen.

The runtime-only bootstrap has been removed. Lobby2 now contains the authored kitchen. The migration was run through the Unity Editor API and the scene was saved. Re-running Dine In > Fast Food > Create Editable Kitchen in Lobby2 validates an existing authored kitchen without rebuilding or replacing edits.

## Where to edit everything

Open Lobby2 and expand the **Fast Food Cooking** root.

- **FastFoodCookingController**: batch size, staff slots, reserve stock and refresh interval, cook/ready/deadline timers, staff speed, assembly time, and every task instruction. The old assignment-ratio field is hidden and no longer governs ownership.
- **Fast Food Kitchen HUD**: edit the actual Canvas, panels, labels, buttons, sprites, font sizes, anchors, padding, scroll views and layout groups. Button actions are saved UnityEvents in the Inspector.
- **FastFoodCookingView**: dynamic text formats, stage labels, notices, low-stock threshold, feedback colors/durations, return/placement animation settings, and placement/ready/completion sounds.
- **Grill / Fry / Assembler Station**: move the actual saved camera, surfaces, colliders, food anchors and tray anchors. Edit the station title, colors, ray range and drag-preview position on FastFoodCookingStation. Runtime does not recalculate camera framing or surface transforms.
- **Cooking Positions** under Grill/Fry: edit each saved position's Owner, collider, food anchor, circular timer and particles. Four positions belong to staff and four to the player in each station. Original appliance and oil transforms are preserved.
- **Station surface labels** under the HUD Station: edit each saved label and circular timer directly. Active timers track food anchors; the overall HUD layout remains authored.
- **Editable Templates** and **Assets/_Project/Restaurant/CookingAssets**: ingredient slots, order tickets, ticket product images, cooking-food models, completed servings, drinks, drag previews and materials. These are connected prefab instances; edit prefab assets to change all instances, or keep scene-instance overrides.
- **Recipe assets**: ingredients and quantities, assembly steps, cooking/preparation station, kitchen ingredient icon override, raw-color multiplier, and per-product kitchen cooking/serving/preview prefabs. A custom cooking-food prefab must include a Collider and FastFoodCookingDragHandle.
- **ItemData assets**: raw ingredient icon, units per box, and optional ingredient-specific kitchen preview prefab.
- **Existing task HUD, inventory, staff, save and Restock assets** remain their existing authorities; this migration does not duplicate them.

Live orders, progress counters and food instances are transient gameplay state, not editor-authored orders. They instantiate the saved templates; their layout and appearance are editable. Reservation safety, completion-only consumption and save rules remain code invariants, not switches that can accidentally allow double consumption.

Inactive station roots and panels are intentionally saved in the hierarchy. Select an inactive object to edit its Inspector; enable the desired panel temporarily to work on its layout, then save. Play Mode initializes the normal station selection flow. Do not save tuning edits only during Play Mode.

Use **Dine In > Fast Food > Validate Editable Kitchen** to check the authored references. The migration does not overwrite an existing authored layout.

## Verification

Run **Dine In > Fast Food > Check Cozy Gameplay Regressions** and the existing batch regression check in the Editor for ownership, four-item player capacity, parallel staff production, sandwich handoff, exact final consumption and saved scene references. **Apply Cozy Gameplay Polish** is an explicit authoring/migration command, not a runtime bootstrap; it saves Lobby2 and assets and should not be used as an automatic layout reset.

Run the isolated gameplay checks:

    powershell -File tools/verify_fastfood_cooking.ps1

Run the existing persistence checks:

    python tools/verify_fastfood_persistence.py --unity "D:/Unity/Unity/Hub/Editor/6000.0.40f1/Editor"

The gameplay harness compiles the actual cooking ledger and task broker with small asset/platform adapters. It verifies reservations, completion-only consumption, burns, cancellation, contention, staff automation, handoff, drink filling, duplicates, deadlines, remakes, retry after restocking, and guidance filtering. It does not simulate Unity lifecycle, rendering, navigation, or touch input.

The authoring migration was compiled in the connected Unity Editor. Scene references, persistent button bindings and saved prefab references were checked through the Editor API. The gameplay harness has 30 scenarios, including pre-entry staff completion, occupied Assembler queues, partial-prep handoff, and explicit station/ingredient overrides. These checks are separate from a full shift or device playthrough.

Perspective polish is saved by the explicit Editor menu **Dine In > Fast Food > Apply Perspective Kitchen Polish**; do not run it as a runtime initializer. Edit **Floating Kitchen Cue**, **Previous/Next Station**, **Station Transition Fade**, the ticket's **Ticket Motion / Order Progress Track**, and each station's camera/food anchors directly. The new toast and view-polish components expose durations, rise, colors, ghost material and transitions in the Inspector. The three drink prefabs and their glass/liquid materials are in **CookingAssets/Batch**. The active Kitchen HUD Canvas is saved enabled; only its selection/station panels toggle during play.

2026-09-25 verification: 30 isolated ledger/guidance scenarios and the Unity batch/cozy saved-content regressions passed. A Play Mode fixture exercised actual view drag/drop callbacks for five Assembler products including all three glass drinks, Serve, both saved station-arrow events, fryer loading, cooked-food pickup and camera transition, prep-table collection, and Exit during a fade. It used isolated ledger data without consuming campaign inventory. Assembler and fryer-prep captures were visually reviewed. Fixed issues found during verification included an inactive saved HUD Canvas, a divider-obscured assembler viewpoint, a duplicated scene ticket motion override, product-row clipping, and raw fryer loading incorrectly triggering the prep camera. Full campaign/day completion, touch input and device builds were not tested.

## Manual acceptance checklist

1. Start Lobby2, take/pay for orders, and select Help Kitchen. Confirm only one focused station is visible and the lobby HUD restores after Exit.
2. At Grill, load four patties, watch cooking and the prep camera transition, add recipe layers, then finish any remaining batch. Check that recipe costs are deducted only after each complete product. Confirm staff use only the left grill.
3. At Fry, fill the four player positions on the right two wells; complete one serving and burn/discard another. Check the oil depth, subtle bubbles, staff on the left wells and sandwich-filling handoff. Unfinished burnt food has no consumption charge.
4. At Assembler, inspect pictured tickets, fill the player tray and drink, and Serve once. Verify dine-in delivery and takeaway pickup still work.
5. Leave during cooking and partial assembly. Confirm staff finish the same work while other stations continue.
6. Toggle task guidance, inspect restock notices, and navigate to Restock. Check cancelled drags do not lose stock.
7. Verify camera positions against actual equipment, readable HUDs, long orders, mouse/touch dragging, and supported screen sizes.
8. Complete/restart a day and verify raw/finished stock and finance recovery.

Unity playthrough, mobile/device checks, camera/layout visual verification and build deployment must be reported separately from the isolated checks. They have not been established by the harness.
